{"version":"1","id":"engineyard","handle":"engineyard","name":"Engine Yard","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/f8bc95efc264465baf588f5cca2cef5b","description":null,"is_squad":false,"x":-6.284,"y":6.4916,"cluster_id":3,"posts_collected":17,"newest_post_at":"2023-03-02T17:07:06.000Z","oldest_post_at":"2021-05-26T12:00:00.000Z","features":{"hype_score":0.0,"listicle_ratio":0.0,"question_ratio":0.058824,"avg_read_time":3.625,"summary_length_avg":319.428571,"title_length_avg":42.058824,"title_length_var":188.878893,"tag_entropy":2.587836,"tag_diversity":1.058824,"top_tag_share":0.235294,"avg_upvotes":0.352941,"median_upvotes":0.0,"avg_comments":0.0,"comment_to_upvote_ratio":0.142857,"zero_engagement_share":0.764706,"viral_share":0.0,"posts_per_week":0.179033,"non_article_ratio":0.0,"author_present_share":0.352941,"recency_skew":0.133333},"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[-6.6912,-9.0147],"cadence_depth":[-8.6471,-5.4853],"hype_variety":[-8.2059,-5.75],"title_style":[6.5,-7.1029]},"top_tags":[["rails",9],["aws",2],["nginx",2],["general-programming",2],["devops",2],["infrastructure",2],["containers",2],["graviton",1]],"sample_titles":{"representative":["Rewrite Rules in Nginx","That\u2019s Not a Memory Leak, It\u2019s Bloat","That's Not a Memory Leak, It's Bloat"],"outlier":["Containerisation in the enterprise - Engine Yard: Container fun with Bob and Alice"]},"recent_posts":[{"id":"IWQss18jG","title":"Engine Yard Cloud: Pricing Update","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/engine-yard-cloud-pricing-update/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/engine-yard-cloud-pricing-update-iwqss18jg","summary":"Starting April 1st 2023 our prices will be changing. These changes enable us to reinvest back into the Engine Yard product.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/c237ad0f29f283d90f6df014b50b7eaa","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2023-03-02T17:07:06.000Z"},{"id":"cjWaelC6W","title":"Engine Yard stack v7 is now generally available \u2013 EngineYard","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/engine-yard-stack-v7-is-now-generally-available/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/engine-yard-stack-v7-is-now-generally-available-engineyard-cjwaelc6w","summary":"Engine Yard stack v7 brings the latest technology, databases and processors together so that you can run your applications faster and cheaper. Stack v7 supports Ubuntu 20.04 LTS and it will soon support 22.04. Older stacks such as v5 and v4 run OS versions that have already reached EOL.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/46a279f828afbdf7621f61bc8b1d2e65","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2022-06-23T16:34:19.000Z"},{"id":"HNFT7pS_S","title":"Engine Yard Innovation Event \u2013 EngineYard","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/engine-yard-innovation-event/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/engine-yard-innovation-event-engineyard-hnft7ps-s","summary":null,"image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/c237ad0f29f283d90f6df014b50b7eaa","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2022-04-18T12:31:25.000Z"},{"id":"bzD6Wq2Ug","title":"That's Not a Memory Leak, It's Bloat","url":"https://blog.engineyard.com/thats-not-a-memory-leak-its-bloat","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/that-s-not-a-memory-leak-it-s-bloat-bzd6wq2ug","summary":"Bloated mongrels are 2-5x larger than they should be, or spike in size suddenly after a certain subset of requests. The root cause is not the server, but the code the server is running for you. The easiest way to detect bloat is to watch the Application Server process.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/2c733abffac9c3f48b719c641186faa0","num_upvotes":1,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2022-01-12T17:42:04.016Z"},{"id":"hSuvyCpzM","title":"That\u2019s Not a Memory Leak, It\u2019s Bloat","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/thats-not-a-memory-leak-its-bloat/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/that-s-not-a-memory-leak-it-s-bloat-hsuvycpzm","summary":"Bloated mongrels are 2-5x larger than they should be, or spike in size suddenly. The root cause is not the server, but the code the server is running for you. The easiest way to detect bloat is to watch the Application Server process size.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/df657ca30f73bada8ddddd5cb5337842","num_upvotes":1,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2022-01-12T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"XtFNRYe4F","title":"Evolution of Encrypted Credentials in Rails 6.2","url":"https://blog.engineyard.com/encrypted-credentials-in-rails-6.2-evolution","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/evolution-of-encrypted-credentials-in-rails-6-2-xtfnrye4f","summary":"The concept of encrypted secrets evolved and acquired a better shape with each update of the Rails version. The latest update made it to separate credential files for each environment. This built-in feature necessitates separate encryption key for each credential file, thus guaranteeing more security. Let us analyze and discuss encrypted credentials further, how to read them, and how to manage a secret key base.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/4160ef8ca875f2dbde1364e33e01dd51","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2021-10-27T10:42:22.740Z"},{"id":"EHmG9CGfS","title":"Evolution of Encrypted Credentials in Rails 6.2","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/encrypted-credentials-in-rails-6-2-evolution/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/evolution-of-encrypted-credentials-in-rails-6-2-ehmg9cgfs","summary":"Evolution of Encrypted credentials Encrypted secrets were introduced with Rails 5.1 in a view to bringing more security to the secrets handled. In this version, these were referred to as \u2018secrets\u2019 and were referenced by The secrets.yml.enc file. This file handles the secrets along with an encryption key.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1679501371/4b9828296de47992b32087c69ada50e6","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2021-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"biEbuM7YB","title":"Rewrite Rules in Nginx","url":"https://blog.engineyard.com/rewrite-rules-nginx","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/rewrite-rules-in-nginx-biebum7yb","summary":"Rewrite rules modify a part or whole of the URL. This is done for two reasons. First, to inform clients about the relocation of resources, and second, to control the flow to Nginx. The two general-purpose methods used widely for rewriting URLs are the return directive and the rewrite directive. The rewrite directive is more powerful.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/e1b09f2e7b02dc53f9feb34ca1d433a2","num_upvotes":3,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2021-10-20T17:55:27.325Z"},{"id":"j_2t4xZa4","title":"Rewrite Rules in Nginx","url":"https://www.engineyard.com/blog/rewrite-rules-nginx/","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/rewrite-rules-in-nginx-j-2t4xza4","summary":"Knowing how to create the Nginx rewrite rules can save a lot of your effort and time. The two general-purpose methods used widely for rewriting URLs are the return directive and the rewrite directive. Rewrite rules modify a part or whole of the URL. This is done for two reasons: to inform clients about the relocation of resources, and second, to control the flow to Nginx.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/c6bf3e72dd4a72785531e0952fa42bde","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2021-10-20T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"K2HIoLN83","title":"Code Concurrency and Two Easy Fixes","url":"https://blog.engineyard.com/code-concurrency-and-fixes","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/code-concurrency-and-two-easy-fixes-k2hioln83","summary":"Executors separate the framework and application codes by wrapping the application code. The code that is to be protected is wrapped before another request is hit. A Reloader is employed when there is a need to reload the application. When the requested condition asks for reloading, the Reloader delays the application reload until it is secure.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/a26dedfa965bd3533d27fbd6c686df8f","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2021-10-12T06:12:21.533Z"}],"similar_handles":["sitepen","shiftmag","helios","kittygiraudel","syncfusion"],"opposite_handles":["felixrieseberg","xai","hamelhusain","hwchase17","skirano"],"similar":[{"id":"sitepen","handle":"sitepen","name":"SitePen","is_squad":false,"x":-6.6564,"y":6.9328,"cluster_id":2,"posts_collected":300,"image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1667134932/logos/sitepen","features_preview":{"posts_per_week":0.693022,"median_upvotes":0.0,"author_present_share":0.65,"hype_score":0.026667,"avg_read_time":4.525896,"tag_diversity":0.296667},"top_tags_preview":[["general-programming",96],["javascript",89],["architecture",45]],"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[2.0882,1.5588],"cadence_depth":[5.5882,-3.4853],"hype_variety":[6.1912,-0.2206],"title_style":[6.6765,6.6471]}},{"id":"shiftmag","handle":"shiftmag","name":"ShiftMag - Insightful Engineering Content","is_squad":true,"x":-6.7278,"y":5.5685,"cluster_id":2,"posts_collected":221,"image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--IO-OIwVf--/f_auto/v1724242797/squads/shiftmag","features_preview":{"posts_per_week":1.43539,"median_upvotes":2.0,"author_present_share":0.538462,"hype_score":0.03125,"avg_read_time":4.421569,"tag_diversity":0.751131},"top_tags_preview":[["ai",52],["productivity",47],["career",33]],"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[6.3824,8.2059],"cadence_depth":[3.4265,-3.6912],"hype_variety":[6.8529,8.8824],"title_style":[9.3382,6.4559]}},{"id":"helios","handle":"helios","name":"Helios","is_squad":false,"x":-6.8245,"y":5.4998,"cluster_id":2,"posts_collected":63,"imag